;; vec-of-any-repr-decision-ice: a `(Vec any)` used to ABORT the compiler.
;;
;;   tur: internal error (ICE): a representation decision disagrees with
;;   repr_of at binding.
;;     repr-shadow binding let-bind type=(type-app Vec any)
;;                 want=heap-ptr got=carrier-i64 cty=int64_t own=int64_t
;;
;; Two questions were being answered inconsistently, and they are different
;; questions:
;;
;;   * Does `(Vec any)` NAME a monomorph?  `any` c-names to `tur_tagged_t`, so
;;     it is as nameable as `(Vec int)` -- but the concrete-layout table
;;     rejects TY_ANY (a 16-byte by-value FIELD is an ABI change), so the type
;;     argument failed every concreteness test and the let binder fell to the
;;     int64 carrier while repr_of said typed heap pointer.  Admitted in
;;     adt_app_type_arg_is_concrete, which is the predicate that asks exactly
;;     this, leaving the layout table alone.
;;
;;   * How is an `any` ELEMENT stored?  A container slot is one machine word
;;     and a tur_tagged_t is two, so erasing it keeps the payload and drops the
;;     tag.  repr_of now answers REPR_BOXED_AGG at REPR_POS_CONTAINER_ELEM --
;;     the same answer a by-value aggregate element has had since increment 4,
;;     which brings the store, the read and the element-free with it
;;     (type_is_boxed_container_elem IS that call).
;;
;; A third defect shared the symptom and is fixed here too: `vec-of`'s macro
;; binds the first element as a TYPE WITNESS and pushes the same value, so an
;; owned `any` was used twice -- TUR-E0201 "cannot copy unique value '__vw'"
;; raised inside stdlib/vec.tur.  The witness is never read, only its type is,
;; so the parameter is `^borrow`.
;;
;; All three routes to a `(Vec any)` are covered below: a call returning `any`
;; (the reported repro), an in-place ascription (which used to trip the
;; TUR-E0201 instead), and an explicitly-ascribed `(vec-new)`.
;;
;; WHAT THIS DOES NOT YET DO: `vec-get` on a `(Vec any)` still reports its
;; result as `int` rather than `any`, so an element cannot be read back with
;; its tag -- `(type-of (vec-get v 0))` is a compile error naming that. The
;; element type does not flow through the container. That is S6's subject and
;; is filed as vec-get-on-a-vec-any-does-not-return-any; it is why this
;; fixture asserts lengths rather than element types.

(defn dyn [x : any] : any x)

(defn main [] : int
  ;; 1. The reported repro: an element from a call returning `any`.
  (println (vec-len (vec-of (dyn 1))))
  ;; 2. An element ascribed to `any` in place.
  (println (vec-len (vec-of (:: 2 any))))
  ;; 3. An explicitly ascribed empty vector, pushed into.
  (let [v (:: (vec-new) (Vec any))]
    (vec-push! v (dyn 3))
    (vec-push! v (dyn "four"))
    (println (vec-len v))
    ;; The element boxes are owned by the vector and released with it: the
    ;; store side boxes them because repr_of says BOXED_AGG, and vec-free's
    ;; `tur-vec-elem-wide?` fold reads the same predicate, so the two cannot
    ;; drift.  requires.leak-check is what holds that lockstep.
    (vec-free v))
  0)
